        "id": "ch6_1_1-1",
        "text": "FTP (Port 21)\nThe File Transfer Protocol (FTP) is used to transfer files between a client and a\nremote server. The latter is used to stock files so you can access them remotely.\nSometimes FTP is used by web applications to synchronize the hosted source\ncode (e.g., HTML, JavaScript, etc.). Two secure implementations of FTP are\nFTPS and SFTP. Secure File Transfer Protocol (SFTP) uses the SSH protocol to\ntransmit files (by default, it uses the same port 22 of SSH). On the other hand,\nthe File Transfer Protocol Secure (FTPS) uses SSL to encrypt the file transfer,\nand it uses ports 989 and 990 for this purpose.\nThese are the common weaknesses in the FTP protocol:\nI\nI Login credentials are sent in cleartext.\nI\nI File transmission is not encrypted.\nEsta secci\u00f3n contiene las siguientes subsecciones\n\u2022 Exploitation Scenarios for an FTP Server\n\u2022 Enumeration Workflow\n\u2022 Service Scan\n\u2022 Advanced Scripting Scan with Nmap\n\u2022 More Brute-Forcing Techniques\nSi deseas explorar cualquiera de estas subsecciones, puedes pregunt\u00e1rmelo directamente.",
